  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">BUILDING THE SUSTAINABILITY OF NEOTROPICAL GRASSLANDS</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Grassland-dependent bird species have declined more than any other avian group in North America and are the most threatened group of migratory birds in South America. These declines are being driven by habitat loss due to agricultural expansion and intensification, over-grazing, and altered fire regimes. Fortunately, many declining grassland species can co-exist with bird-friendly agricultural practices. This project will enhance conservation of neotropical and resident grassland dependent birds throughout Latin American through the development, documentation and dissemination of best management practices and the completion of the Latin American component of a grasslands conservation business plan. Benefitted species include Swainsons hawk, American golden plover, upland sandpiper, buff- breasted sandpiper, dickcissel, and bobolink...</narrative>
  </description>
